Here are all four styles in one method, each suited to a different need.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class OrderProcessor {
    public void processOrders(List<Order> orders) {
        // 1. for-each — clean, read-only access
        for (Order order : orders) {
            System.out.println(order.id());
        }

        // 2. Iterator — safe removal during iteration
        Iterator<Order> it = orders.iterator();
        while (it.hasNext()) {
            Order order = it.next();
            if (order.amount() < 10) {
                it.remove();              // the iterator-coordinated removal when supported
            }
        }

        // 3. ListIterator — bidirectional traversal
        ListIterator<Order> back = orders.listIterator(orders.size());
        while (back.hasPrevious()) {
            System.out.println(back.previous().item());
        }

        // 4. forEach with a lambda — functional pass
        orders.forEach(order -> System.out.println(order.customer()));
    }
}

record Order(Long id, String item, String customer, double amount) {}
```

The for-each and `forEach` read cleanly, the `Iterator` deletes safely, and the `ListIterator` walks backward — one collection, four tools.

Choose the view that already contains the data the loop needs.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class MapIteration {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<Long, String> orders = new HashMap<>();
        orders.put(101L, "Laptop");
        orders.put(102L, "Mouse");

        // Both parts: each entry already contains its key and value.
        for (var entry : orders.entrySet()) {
            System.out.println(entry.getKey() + " -> " + entry.getValue());
        }

        // A concise alternative for a simple action.
        orders.forEach((id, item) -> System.out.println(id + ": " + item));

        // This works, but get(id) is an avoidable lookup when both parts are needed.
        for (Long id : orders.keySet()) {
            String item = orders.get(id);
            System.out.println(id + " => " + item);
        }

        for (Long id : orders.keySet()) System.out.println(id);
        for (String item : orders.values()) System.out.println(item);
    }
}
```

The third loop does one extra lookup per key. It does not perform two hash lookups merely by walking `keySet()`.

```java
import java.util.*;

List<String> items = new ArrayList<>(List.of("a", "b", "c"));
Iterator<String> it = items.iterator();
while (it.hasNext()) {
    String s = it.next();
    if (s.equals("b")) it.remove();   // safe removal — the only edit available
}
// Cannot go backward, cannot set() or add() — Iterator is one direction.
System.out.println(items);            // [a, c]
```

```java
import java.util.*;

List<String> items = new ArrayList<>(List.of("a", "b", "c"));

// Reverse traversal: start at the end, walk with previous()
ListIterator<String> back = items.listIterator(items.size());
while (back.hasPrevious()) {
    String s = back.previous();
    System.out.println(back.nextIndex() + " -> " + s); // index tracking
}

// Edit in place during a forward pass
ListIterator<String> fwd = items.listIterator();
while (fwd.hasNext()) {
    String s = fwd.next();
    if (s.equals("b")) fwd.set("B");   // replace last returned element
    if (s.equals("c")) fwd.add("d");   // insert at current cursor position
}
System.out.println(items);             // [a, B, c, d]
```

Reach for `ListIterator` only on a `List`, and only when you need reverse traversal or in-place `set()`/`add()`.

The unsupported form is best described as **may throw**, because fail-fast detection is best effort.

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.CopyOnWriteArrayList;

public class OrderFilter {
    static void unsupportedChange(List<String> orders) {
        for (String order : orders) {
            if (order.equals("cancelled")) {
                orders.remove(order); // may invalidate this iterator and cause CME
            }
        }
    }

    static void removeWithIterator(List<String> orders) {
        Iterator<String> it = orders.iterator();
        while (it.hasNext()) {
            if (it.next().equals("cancelled")) {
                it.remove(); // supported by ArrayList's iterator
            }
        }
    }

    static void removeWithPredicate(List<String> orders) {
        orders.removeIf("cancelled"::equals);
    }

    static void snapshotExample(CopyOnWriteArrayList<String> orders) {
        for (String order : orders) {
            orders.remove(order); // loop still sees its earlier snapshot
        }
    }
}
```

`CopyOnWriteArrayList` is useful only for read-heavy cases: every successful write copies its backing array.
